Z||000005||84fierotrevor||11-12-2001||08:45 PM||||Ok i taqlked to him again he said ok fine he will stick it on for me but it's gonna cost me alot and might not work he said. and if it does it will overheat and blow because it<BR>doesn't have the water hole !<BR>should i have him put it on ?<BR>are we shure it will fit ?<BR>does any one know anything about wether or not there is even a place for the water to go thrue the head of the engine ?<BR>im so !@#$ lost.||||reg||1
Z||000006||avengador1||11-12-2001||09:40 PM||||I don't have the 4 cyl engine, but I looked at my Haynes, Chilton, and GM service manual and from what I can tell your thermostat is mounted on the side of the head, and the head gasket water holes seem to be near the exterior of the gasket. If you put the gasket on the head you should be able to see where the water passages are and do the same to the block to see if they match up. There seem to be 4 oval shaped water passages in the gasket and some round ones too set in a triangular formation(between the cylinders). From experience I can tell you that some gaskets cover some of these passages to redirect flow. In summary, if the head bolts up to the block and the water passages line up it should work.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Countach||11-13-2001||01:46 AM||||Go to a junk yard and start pulling heads until you find one that matchs.  Start with the same year car, all it costs you is time, and a few dollars.  I think your mechanic has a point.||||reg||1
Z||000008||84fierotrevor||11-13-2001||02:09 AM||||Are you sure it wont fit ?<BR>cuss alota people on here said it will.<BR>I guess im just gonna find out cuss i am paying him to put it on.<BR>If it doesnt fit im just gonna part out my fiero and keep riding my damn biclycle or somthing i dunno.||||reg||1
Z||000009||JohnF||11-13-2001||07:16 AM||||You state the head is off the same model Fiero.  Maybe, maybe not!  Some things change as a result of owner actions before you see the car.  Some things change during production as a result of factory changes.<P>If the water jacket gasket fits the block, fine.  If it fits the head, fine.  If it doesn't fit the head - don't use the head!  Get one that matches the gasket and the block.  <P>If water can't flow between the head and block unrestricted, you have an air-cooled engine.  Fieros were not desiogned for that!||||reg||9
Z||000010||theogre||11-13-2001||12:48 PM||||In many cases, Not all holes in the head/block are meant to be used. Some are meant only as drain holes for casting sand.<P>Does the head hole in question have a matching hole in both the new gasket and the block?<P>Federal Mogul covers this on their Felpro pages. <A HREF="http://www.federalmogul.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.federalmogul.com/</A>  Or at least they did last time I looked. haven't been there in awhile.<P>Gm has made allot of revisions to the heads in the middle of runs. Will it run hot because of a missing hole? maybe/maybe not. It's entirely posible the hole was deleted because that area of the head or block needed more heat. This is just a posible as the hole being added to reduce heat there... <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000011||ITB 2M4||11-13-2001||01:03 PM||||I have an '84 Fiero that I'm racing in the SCCA, with the 2.5L Iron Duke.  I'm using the head off of a Chevy Luv pickup from '78.  I havn't had any problems what-so-ever with cooling.  I ran at Sebring last month, without putting the front spoiler on, and the car ran 180 degrees during the race.  Granted, it was 70 degrees out, but the motor was routinely over 5,000rpm, or at least getting there in a hurry.<P>I've also noticed that some of the head gaskets I use have a hole in one corner, while other's don't.  I've used both, and have had no problems at all.<P>If nothing else, when he gets it all back together, watch the temp. guage very closely.  If it just keeps climbing, changing out a head is very easy to do.  It can be done in the driveway with hand tools.  But I think you should be fine.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4||||reg||10
